{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":13703816,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"pest-control\",\"short_title\":\"control-cutworms-vegetable-gardens-new-lawn-seedlings\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-08-24 00:23:47\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-08-24 00:23:47\",\"author\":\"Robert Hayes\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/Afbf19ab7f3514cef87bc96fa2e302019s.jpg\",\"title\":\"How to Control Cutworms in Vegetable Gardens & New Lawn Seedlings\",\"description\":\"Learn how to identify, prevent, and control cutworms damaging your vegetable garden and new lawn seedlings with proven DIY methods and expert tips.\",\"url\":\"/pest-control/control-cutworms-vegetable-gardens-new-lawn-seedlings\",\"body\":\"<p>If you have ever planted a row of vegetable transplants only to find them severed at the soil line the next morning, or watched patches of newly seeded lawn wither and collapse without explanation, cutworms may be the culprit. These sneaky caterpillars are among the most destructive pests facing home gardeners and lawn owners, and they strike under the cover of darkness, making them difficult to catch in the act. Understanding what cutworms are, how they operate, and what you can do to stop them is essential for protecting both your <a href=\\\"/vegetable-gardening\\\">vegetable garden</a> and your <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/new-lawn-seedlings\\\">new lawn seedlings</a>.</p>\\n\\n<p>Cutworms are not picky eaters. They attack a wide range of plants, from tender tomato seedlings and pepper transplants to freshly germinated grass blades. Their feeding habit — chewing through stems at or just below the soil surface — gives them their name and makes them particularly devastating to young plants that have not yet established strong root systems. Whether you are starting a spring vegetable garden or trying to establish a lush new lawn from seed, knowing how to control cutworms can mean the difference between a thriving landscape and a frustrating season of replanting.</p>\\n\\n<p>This comprehensive guide covers everything you need to know about cutworm identification, prevention strategies, hands-on control methods, and when it may be time to bring in professional help. By the end, you will have a clear action plan for defending your garden and lawn from these nocturnal pests.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Identifying Cutworms: What They Look Like and the Damage They Cause</h2>\\n\\n<p>Before you can effectively control cutworms, you need to confirm that they are actually the problem. Cutworms are the larval stage of several species of noctuid moths, and there are dozens of species active across different regions. Despite the variety, they share common characteristics and behaviors that make identification relatively straightforward once you know what to look for.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Physical Description of Cutworms</h3>\\n\\n<p>Cutworms are smooth, fleshy caterpillars that typically range from one to two inches in length when fully grown. Their coloring varies by species but generally includes shades of brown, gray, green, or black, often with subtle striping or mottling along their bodies. The most distinctive behavioral clue is their tendency to curl into a tight C-shape when disturbed — a reflex that helps distinguish them from other soil-dwelling larvae such as grubs or wireworms.</p>\\n\\n<p>During the day, cutworms hide just below the soil surface, usually within the top inch or two, near the base of plants they have been feeding on. To find them, gently dig around the base of a freshly damaged plant, and you will likely discover one or more curled caterpillars lurking in the soil.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Recognizing Cutworm Damage in Vegetable Gardens</h3>\\n\\n<p>Cutworm damage in vegetable gardens is unmistakable once you learn the signs. The classic symptom is a seedling or transplant that has been cleanly severed at or slightly below the soil line, as though snipped with scissors. The toppled plant often lies nearby, still green and fresh, because the cutworm typically feeds on the stem rather than consuming the entire plant.</p>\\n\\n<p>Some species of cutworms are known as climbing cutworms, which climb up plants to feed on leaves and buds, leaving irregular holes in foliage. Others tunnel into fruits and vegetables that sit on or near the soil surface. However, the stem-cutting behavior at the soil line is by far the most common and most damaging, particularly to young transplants of tomatoes, peppers, cabbage, broccoli, beans, and corn.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Recognizing Cutworm Damage in New Lawn Seedlings</h3>\\n\\n<p>In newly seeded lawns, cutworm damage can be more subtle and is sometimes mistaken for disease, drought stress, or poor germination. Look for irregular brown or thinning patches in areas where grass seedlings have emerged but then suddenly collapse. Upon close inspection, you may find that individual grass blades have been chewed off at the base, and the soil surface may show tiny tunnels or loosened soil where cutworms have burrowed.</p>\\n\\n<p>Because new <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-seeding-tips\\\">lawn seedlings</a> are extremely tender and have minimal energy reserves, even a small population of cutworms can cause significant patchy damage across a freshly seeded area. The damage is often most noticeable in the early morning, after a night of feeding, and may appear to spread rapidly if left unchecked.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Common Cutworm Species to Know</h3>\\n\\n<p>While there are many species, a few are particularly problematic for home gardeners and lawn owners. The black cutworm (<em>Agrotis ipsilon</em>) is one of the most widespread and destructive, attacking both vegetables and turfgrass. The variegated cutworm (<em>Peridroma saucia</em>) is a climbing species that damages foliage as well as stems. The army cutworm (<em>Euxoa auxiliaris</em>) can appear in large numbers and cause widespread defoliation. The dingy cutworm (<em>Feltia jaculifera</em>) is especially common in lawns and feeds heavily on grass seedlings. Knowing which species is active in your area can help you time your control efforts more precisely, though the management strategies described below are effective across species.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Preventing Cutworms Before They Become a Problem</h2>\\n\\n<p>As with most pest management, prevention is always more effective and less labor-intensive than trying to control an active infestation. Cutworms follow predictable life cycles and environmental preferences, which gives you several opportunities to disrupt them before they reach your plants.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Cultivate and Prepare Soil Before Planting</h3>\\n\\n<p>Cutworms lay their eggs in weedy, grassy areas, and the larvae overwinter in the soil. Tilling or cultivating your garden beds in early spring, at least two to three weeks before planting, exposes overwintering larvae and pupae to predators and harsh weather conditions, significantly reducing their numbers. This is especially important if you are converting a section of lawn or a weedy area into a new vegetable garden, as these sites tend to harbor the highest cutworm populations.</p>\\n\\n<p>For new lawn areas, thorough <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/soil-preparation-for-lawns\\\">soil preparation</a> before seeding serves a dual purpose: it creates an ideal seedbed for grass germination while simultaneously disrupting cutworm habitats. Rake the area thoroughly, remove plant debris, and allow the soil to settle for a week or two before sowing seed.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Eliminate Weeds and Grass Around Garden Borders</h3>\\n\\n<p>Weeds and grasses serve as primary egg-laying sites for adult cutworm moths. Keeping a clean, weed-free buffer zone around your vegetable garden — at least three to four feet wide — reduces the number of eggs laid in the immediate vicinity of your crops. This is particularly important along fence lines, ditches, and other edges where grasses tend to accumulate.</p>\\n\\n<p>For lawn seedings, keep adjacent areas mowed short and free of tall grasses and weeds during the germination and establishment period. The less attractive the surrounding habitat is to egg-laying moths, the fewer cutworms will find their way to your new seedlings.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Time Your Planting Strategically</h3>\\n\\n<p>Cutworm activity typically peaks in late spring and early summer, coinciding with the emergence of the first generation of larvae from overwintered pupae and the hatching of eggs laid by early-season moths. Where possible, delay transplanting tender vegetable seedlings until cutworm activity begins to decline in your region. Alternatively, start seeds indoors and transplant larger, more resilient seedlings that can better withstand minor feeding damage.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Encourage Natural Predators</h3>\\n\\n<p>A healthy, biodiverse garden and lawn ecosystem includes many natural enemies of cutworms. Ground beetles, parasitic wasps, tachinid flies, birds, and toads all prey on cutworms at various life stages. You can encourage these beneficial organisms by maintaining <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/organic-lawn-care\\\">organic lawn care</a> practices, avoiding broad-spectrum pesticides that kill beneficial insects alongside pests, providing water sources for birds and toads, and leaving some undisturbed habitat areas in your landscape.</p>\\n\\n<p>Planting nectar-rich flowers such as dill, fennel, yarrow, and sweet alyssum near your vegetable garden attracts parasitic wasps and tachinid flies, both of which are important natural controls for cutworm populations.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Use Cover Crops and Crop Rotation</h3>\\n\\n<p>In vegetable gardens, rotating crops and using cover crops can help break cutworm life cycles. Avoid planting susceptible crops in the same location year after year, and consider using winter cover crops such as clover or rye that can be tilled under in spring to disrupt overwintering larvae. A well-planned <a href=\\\"/vegetable-gardening/crop-rotation-guide\\\">crop rotation strategy</a> benefits your soil health while simultaneously reducing pest pressure.</p>\\n\\n<h2>DIY Solutions for Controlling Cutworms</h2>\\n\\n<p>When prevention alone is not enough, or when you discover active cutworm damage in your garden or lawn, a variety of do-it-yourself control methods can help you manage the problem effectively. These range from simple physical barriers to biological controls and targeted organic treatments.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Physical Barriers and Collars</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the oldest and most reliable methods for protecting individual vegetable transplants from cutworms is the use of physical collars. A collar is simply a cylinder placed around the base of a plant, extending about one to two inches into the soil and two to three inches above the soil surface. This prevents cutworms from reaching the stem.</p>\\n\\n<p>You can make effective collars from a variety of household materials. Cardboard toilet paper or paper towel tubes cut into sections work well and decompose naturally over the season. Aluminum foil strips wrapped around the base of plants, plastic cups with the bottoms removed, or even rings of stiff newspaper can serve the same purpose. Place collars at the time of transplanting, pressing them firmly into the soil so there are no gaps through which a cutworm could squeeze.</p>\\n\\n<p>For larger plantings, some gardeners lay sheets of newspaper or cardboard between rows as a mulch barrier, which makes it harder for cutworms to move between plants. This method also helps retain soil moisture and suppress weeds, providing multiple benefits for your <a href=\\\"/vegetable-gardening/vegetable-garden-mulching\\\">vegetable garden</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Hand-Picking and Night Patrols</h3>\\n\\n<p>Because cutworms feed primarily at night, one of the most effective — and often overlooked — control methods is simply going out after dark with a flashlight and collecting them by hand. Walk through your garden rows or across your lawn seedbed about two to three hours after sunset, and scan the soil surface and lower stems of plants. You will often find cutworms actively feeding and can pick them off directly.</p>\\n\\n<p>Drop collected cutworms into a container of soapy water to kill them, or crush them underfoot. While this method is labor-intensive, it can dramatically reduce populations in a small garden or lawn area within just a few evenings of consistent effort. It is especially useful during the first two weeks after transplanting or seeding, when plants are most vulnerable.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Toilet Paper Tube and Stick Barriers</h3>\\n\\n<p>A simple variation on the collar method involves placing a wooden stick, nail, or toothpick directly next to the stem of each transplant, pushed an inch or two into the soil. The theory is that cutworms, which wrap their bodies around a stem to chew through it, are physically prevented from completing this wrapping motion when a rigid object is positioned immediately beside the stem. While not as foolproof as a full collar, this method is extremely quick to implement and provides a meaningful level of protection when combined with other strategies.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Biological Controls: Beneficial Nematodes and Bt</h3>\\n\\n<p>Beneficial nematodes, specifically species in the genus <em>Steinernema</em>, are microscopic worms that you apply to the soil as a liquid drench. These nematodes actively seek out and infect soil-dwelling insects, including cutworm larvae, releasing bacteria that kill the host within one to two days. Nematodes are completely safe for plants, humans, pets, and beneficial insects above ground, making them an excellent choice for integrated pest management in both gardens and lawns.</p>\\n\\n<p>Apply nematodes to moist soil in the evening or on a cloudy day, as they are sensitive to UV light and desiccation. Water the area thoroughly before and after application to help them move through the soil profile. A single application can provide several weeks of control, and repeat applications may be warranted in areas with heavy cutworm pressure.</p>\\n\\n<p><em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em> var. <em>kurstaki</em> (Bt) is a naturally occurring soil bacterium that is toxic to caterpillars when ingested. It is available as a spray or dust and can be applied to plant foliage and the soil surface around susceptible plants. Bt is most effective against younger cutworm larvae, so early application at the first sign of damage is important. It breaks down quickly in sunlight, so apply in the evening and reapply after rain. Bt is safe for humans, pets, birds, and most beneficial insects, making it a cornerstone of <a href=\\\"/pest-control/organic-pest-control-methods\\\">organic pest control</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Diatomaceous Earth and Other Natural Barriers</h3>\\n\\n<p>Food-grade diatomaceous earth (DE) is a fine powder made from the fossilized remains of microscopic aquatic organisms called diatoms. When cutworms crawl across DE, the sharp particles damage their waxy outer cuticle, causing them to dehydrate and die. Sprinkle a ring of DE around the base of transplants or along the edges of lawn seedbeds to create a protective barrier.</p>\\n\\n<p>The primary limitation of diatomaceous earth is that it loses effectiveness when wet, so it must be reapplied after rain or heavy irrigation. Despite this, it remains a popular and affordable option for gardeners seeking a chemical-free approach to cutworm control. Other natural barrier materials include crushed eggshells, coffee grounds, and wood ashes, though their effectiveness is more variable and they require more frequent reapplication.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Organic Insecticides: Spinosad and Pyrethrin</h3>\\n\\n<p>When cutworm populations are high and other methods are insufficient, organic insecticides derived from natural sources can provide stronger control. Spinosad, derived from a soil-dwelling bacterium, is effective against cutworms when applied as a soil drench or foliar spray. It has low toxicity to humans and mammals and is approved for use in organic gardening by most certification bodies.</p>\\n\\n<p>Pyrethrin, derived from chrysanthemum flowers, provides rapid knockdown of cutworms on contact. It breaks down quickly in the environment, reducing the risk of long-term impacts on beneficial insects, but it is toxic to bees and aquatic organisms, so apply it in the evening when bees are not active and avoid runoff into water sources. Both spinosad and pyrethrin should be used as part of a broader management strategy rather than as standalone solutions.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Protecting New Lawn Seedlings from Cutworm Damage</h2>\\n\\n<p>While much of the cutworm discussion in gardening circles focuses on vegetable transplants, new lawn seedlings are equally vulnerable and often receive less attention. Establishing a new lawn from seed represents a significant investment of time, effort, and money, and cutworm damage during the critical germination and early growth stages can set your lawn establishment back by weeks or even require complete reseeding.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Pre-Treatment of Lawn Areas Before Seeding</h3>\\n\\n<p>If you are preparing to seed a new lawn in an area that was previously grassy or weedy, consider pre-treating the soil before sowing seed. Tilling the area thoroughly and allowing it to sit fallow for two to three weeks, with occasional shallow cultivation, can significantly reduce cutworm populations by exposing larvae and pupae to predators and drying conditions. Applying beneficial nematodes to the prepared seedbed about one week before seeding provides an additional layer of protection that will be active when seedlings emerge.</p>\\n\\n<p>Review our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/complete-lawn-seeding-guide\\\">complete lawn seeding guide</a> for detailed instructions on preparing your soil, selecting the right seed, and timing your planting for optimal results.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Monitoring Newly Seeded Lawns</h3>\\n\\n<p>Once your grass seed has germinated and seedlings begin to emerge, monitor the area closely for signs of cutworm activity. Walk the seeded area daily, looking for patches where seedlings appear to have been clipped off at the base or where small brown spots are developing in otherwise healthy green areas. Early detection is critical, as cutworm populations can escalate quickly during the warm, moist conditions that also favor grass germination.</p>\\n\\n<p>If you spot damage, probe the soil around affected areas with your fingers or a small trowel to locate the responsible larvae. Even finding one or two cutworms per square foot indicates a population large enough to cause significant damage to young grass seedlings.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Targeted Treatments for Lawn Seedbeds</h3>\\n\\n<p>For active cutworm infestations in new lawn seedbeds, Bt applied as a spray over the seeded area is one of the safest and most effective options. Because Bt targets only caterpillars and does not harm germinating grass seeds or emerging seedlings, it can be applied without concern for plant damage. Apply in the late afternoon or evening, and repeat applications every five to seven days until damage ceases.</p>\\n\\n<p>Beneficial nematodes are equally well-suited to lawn seedbeds and can be applied through a hose-end sprayer or watering can. Ensure the soil remains consistently moist after application, as nematodes require moisture to survive and move through the soil. This aligns well with the frequent, light irrigation that new lawn seedlings already require.</p>\\n\\n<p>For severe infestations that threaten complete loss of the seeding, a targeted application of spinosad as a soil drench can bring populations under control quickly. Follow label directions carefully, and allow the treated area to dry before allowing children or pets onto the lawn.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Cultural Practices That Reduce Cutworm Pressure in Lawns</h3>\\n\\n<p>Once your new lawn is established, maintaining healthy growing conditions helps grass outgrow and recover from minor cutworm feeding. Proper <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-fertilization-guide\\\">lawn fertilization</a>, consistent mowing at the recommended height for your grass species, and deep, infrequent watering all promote vigorous root growth and dense turf that is more resilient to pest damage. Avoid over-fertilizing with nitrogen, which produces lush, tender growth that is more attractive to cutworms and other pests.</p>\\n\\n<p>Keep the perimeter of your lawn mowed and free of tall weeds and grasses, as these serve as egg-laying sites for adult moths. A well-maintained lawn border acts as a buffer zone, reducing the likelihood that newly hatched larvae will migrate into your turf.</p>\\n\\n<h2>When to Call a Professional for Cutworm Control</h2>\\n\\n<p>Most cutworm problems in home gardens and lawns can be managed effectively with the DIY methods described above. However, there are situations where professional pest control services may be warranted.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Signs That Professional Help Is Needed</h3>\\n\\n<p>Consider reaching out to a licensed pest control professional if you experience any of the following situations. First, if repeated applications of Bt, nematodes, and organic insecticides have failed to bring cutworm damage under control after two to three weeks of consistent effort, a professional may have access to more targeted products and application equipment that are not available to consumers. Second, if cutworm damage is widespread across a large lawn area — more than a few thousand square feet — the logistics of thorough DIY treatment may be impractical, and professional application equipment can ensure even coverage across the entire area. Third, if you are managing a commercial vegetable operation or a high-value landscape installation where the economic stakes of crop or turf loss are significant, professional integrated pest management services can provide monitoring, treatment, and ongoing prevention that goes beyond what most homeowners can accomplish on their own.</p>\\n\\n<h3>What to Expect from Professional Treatment</h3>\\n\\n<p>A professional pest control service will typically begin with a thorough inspection of your garden or lawn to identify the specific cutworm species present, assess population levels, and evaluate environmental conditions that may be contributing to the problem. Based on this assessment, they will develop a treatment plan that may include targeted insecticide applications, biological controls, and cultural recommendations for long-term prevention.</p>\\n\\n<p>Professional applicators have access to products containing active ingredients such as chlorantraniliprole, which is highly effective against cutworms while having minimal impact on beneficial insects, or carbaryl-based baits that target soil-dwelling larvae specifically. They also have the training and equipment to apply these products safely and at the correct rates, reducing the risk of environmental contamination or harm to non-target organisms.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Choosing a Reputable Pest Control Provider</h3>\\n\\n<p>When selecting a pest control professional, look for companies that emphasize integrated pest management (IPM) approaches rather than relying solely on chemical treatments. A good provider will explain their treatment plan clearly, discuss the products they intend to use and their environmental profiles, and offer recommendations for cultural practices you can implement to reduce future pest pressure. Ask about their experience specifically with cutworm management in gardens and lawns, and request references from clients with similar pest challenges.</p>\\n\\n<p>For more information on maintaining a healthy, pest-resistant landscape, explore our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/seasonal-lawn-care-calendar\\\">seasonal lawn care calendar</a> for month-by-month guidance on keeping your lawn vigorous and resilient throughout the growing season.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Cutworm Control</h2>\\n\\n<h3>What time of day are cutworms most active, and when should I look for them?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Cutworms are nocturnal feeders, meaning they are most active during the night, typically emerging from the soil two to three hours after sunset to feed on plant stems and foliage. They retreat back into the soil before dawn, hiding within the top one to two inches of soil near the base of damaged plants. The best time to scout for cutworms is in the evening with a flashlight, or in the early morning by gently digging around the base of freshly damaged plants. During overcast days, cutworms may occasionally feed near the surface, but nighttime patrols are by far the most reliable detection method.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Will cutworms go away on their own if I wait long enough?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Cutworms will eventually complete their larval stage and pupate in the soil, typically within two to four weeks of active feeding, depending on species and temperature. However, waiting for them to go away on their own is rarely a practical strategy, because a single cutworm can destroy dozens of seedlings during its larval period. In vegetable gardens, the damage to young transplants is often irreversible — a severed stem cannot be reattached — and the lost growing time can set your harvest back by weeks. In new lawns, cutworm feeding during the critical establishment phase can result in thin, patchy turf that is vulnerable to weed invasion and erosion. Taking proactive control measures as soon as damage is detected is always preferable to waiting and hoping the problem resolves itself.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Are cutworms the same as grubs, and do the same treatments work for both?</h3>\\n\\n<p>No, cutworms and grubs are different pests with different biology and behavior, although both can be found in soil and both can damage lawns. Cutworms are the larvae of noctuid moths, and they primarily feed on plant stems at or near the soil surface. Grubs are the larvae of beetles such as Japanese beetles and June beetles, and they feed on grass roots below the soil surface, causing turf to lift and roll back like a carpet. While some treatments — particularly beneficial nematodes — can be effective against both pests, many grub-specific treatments such as milky spore are not effective against cutworms, and vice versa. Proper identification is essential before selecting a treatment. For help identifying grub damage, see our guide to <a href=\\\"/pest-control/grub-control-lawns\\\">grub control in lawns</a>.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Can I use coffee grounds or eggshells to repel cutworms effectively?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Coffee grounds and crushed eggshells are popular home remedies that are often recommended for repelling cutworms and other garden pests. While they may provide a modest deterrent effect, research has not consistently demonstrated that they are reliable as standalone control methods. The sharp edges of crushed eggshells and the texture of coffee grounds may discourage cutworms from crossing a barrier, but the effect is temporary and easily disrupted by rain, irrigation, or wind. If you choose to use these materials, apply them in thick rings around individual plants and reapply frequently. For more reliable protection, combine them with proven methods such as collars, Bt, or beneficial nematodes rather than relying on them exclusively.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How do I protect my vegetable transplants without using any chemical or biological products at all?</h3>\\n\\n<p>If you prefer to avoid all chemical and biological products, physical barriers and cultural practices can still provide substantial protection against cutworms. Collars made from cardboard, aluminum foil, or plastic placed around each transplant at the time of planting are highly effective and require no additional inputs. Tilling garden beds well in advance of planting to expose and destroy overwintering larvae, maintaining weed-free borders around garden areas, and conducting nightly hand-picking patrols during the first two to three weeks after transplanting can keep cutworm populations below damaging levels without any purchased products. The key is consistency — physical and cultural methods require more frequent attention than product-based approaches, but they can be equally effective when applied diligently. For more tips on chemical-free gardening, visit our <a href=\\\"/vegetable-gardening/organic-vegetable-gardening\\\">organic vegetable gardening</a> resource.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-08-24 00:27:25\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}